nd it could include  the testing for people coming in it  could include social distancing measures  and we'll make sure as I said  particularly as we consider any  transition to a second phase that we're  absolutely on top of the scientific  evidence and are taking all the measures  that are necessary to protect people's  health to protect a life but also to  preserve our way of life as we go  forward economically and socially now we  are running out of time but you've been  deputizing for the prime minister while  he recovers how is he he's good he's on  good spirits and he's taken the time and  taken the doctor's advice to to rebuild  his strength he's going to be back at  work full-time properly at the helm on  Monday and as you can imagine with the  prime minister he's raring to go  and I spoke to him I spoken to him every  day this week we've made sure that he's  a rest of everything that's going on and  we had a meeting at Chequers and the  Chancellor joined and various members of  the top team so I think he's right to  have taken the time he did to make sure  he can bounce back but he's looking  forward to getting back at the the reins  on Monday okay and now a you of course  have been deputizing for the prime  minister as I said and  you took over from the Prime Minister  when he was incapacitated it's the kind  of thing that I'm sure that you never  expect you will ever have to do in the  circumstances that you did so I'm  interested to know you know how did you  find out that you were going to be doing  this role who told you and has it been  difficult to balance you know the need  to show leadership at this time of  crisis but also of course not really  wanting to I guess do anything that  Boris Johnson wouldn't do he said look  at unprecedented situation the cabinet  secretary and other officials explained  the situation to me my first instinct  was obviously be worried for the Prime  Minister but I knew he was in great  hands it's in Thomas's but frankly with  the country going through this crisis on  its knees and with the Prime Minister  are stricken with a deadly virus the  only thing I was focused on is stepping  up to the plate and not letting my Prime  Minister down and try not let my country  down and the last two weeks have been a  challenge but we've had a great team  effort in cabinet everyone has been  focused on what they've got to do and I  think that they've performed  exceptionally well it's a team effort  and of course the country I think  there's been a real strong unity of  purpose as we go through the this very  difficult period particularly with a  milestone of 20,000 deaths and so I mean  I can only pay tribute to those in the  frontline and the British public for  following the guidance and stepping up  to the plate but my role is a small part  of that well I think we'd certainly  sharing the tribute today's are on the  front line and just very finely and  there's unconfirmed rumors and reports  that kim jeong-hoon the leader of North  Korea has died now your foreign  secretary so I'm sure that your best  place to you know try and answer this  question if anyone can do you know if  he's dead  no we're reports are unincorporated I've  seen the international media report but  we don't have any verified state to play  on that yet but obviously we're  following it very carefully those  reports
 

